{
  "regeln": [
    "Match visual motifs strictly to spoken technical operations; display active data paths, memory allocation diagrams, or terminal execution instead of generic presenter reactions, text slides, or decorative backdrops.",
    "Replace bullet-point summary cards, text headlines, and conceptual diagrams with direct operational proof, such as live terminal output, archive extraction, and active software execution.",
    "Display verified benchmark charts, live throughput counters, or side-by-side device comparisons whenever processing speeds, transfer rates, or hardware capabilities are narrated.",
    "Feature authentic software interface captures and functional screen recordings in action, eliminating placeholder graphics, question-mark boxes, and generic device mockups.",
    "Visualize concrete hardware and system environments by displaying the specific machines, system resource monitors, and physical or virtual data pathways referenced in the script.",
    "Maintain strict visual-narrative subject parity; never display unrelated pricing models or licensing graphics when explaining technical architecture or system performance.",
    "Depict dynamic operational workflows—such as model loading, weight streaming, dependency setup, or live script execution—rather than static menu items or idle dashboard states.",
    "Open scripts with a practical, user-focused problem-solving hook centered on an actionable task rather than generic tool announcements.",
    "Explain technical trade-offs, practical limits, and hardware procedures in plain, workflow-oriented language rather than isolated jargon or disjointed specification lists.",
    "Treat quality review feedback as provisional diagnostic data to correct visual-to-script alignment gaps without asserting unobserved audience behavior or retention outcomes."
